<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Despotate of the Morea</h1><p class="st-meta">1349 CE – 1460 CE · capital Mystras</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Late Byzantine principality based at Mistras in the Peloponnese, a last flowering of Byzantine culture and the home of the philosopher Plethon.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a> regained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mystras">Mistras</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monemvasia">Monemvasia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a> in 1262 from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea</a> and slowly expanded from there. In 1349 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-vi-kantakouzenos">John VI Kantakouzenos</a> made his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-kantakouzenos">Manuel Kantakouzenos</a> despot of the province, giving it a permanent ruler. From 1383 it passed to members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palaiologos-dynasty">Palaiologos</a> family, beginning with Theodore I.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Despots, usually younger sons of the emperor, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mystras">Mistras</a>, whose churches with their frescoes survive. They gradually conquered the Frankish principality, completing it in 1432. Greek, Albanian and Slavic farmers formed the population. The philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gemistos-plethon">Gemistos Plethon</a> proposed radical reforms there. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hexamilion-wall">Hexamilion wall</a> across the Isthmus was rebuilt in 1415 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1453, the despots Thomas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrios-palaiologos">Demetrios Palaiologos</a> quarrelled and faced Albanian revolts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a> in 1460, the last Greek fortresses falling in 1461.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/despotate-of-the-morea.jpg" alt="Despotate of the Morea" width="320" height="231"><figcaption>Image: Cplakidas This file was derived from: Byzantine imperial flag, 14th century (firesteels vesion).svg · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Byzantine_imperial_flag,_14th_century.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1349 CE – 1460 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mystras</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Manuel Kantakouzenos, Theodore II Palaiologos, Constantine Palaiologos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Medieval Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Despotate of Mystras</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1404&amp;focus=despotate-of-the-morea" aria-label="Despotate of the Morea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5240 842 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Despotate of the Morea lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5560 1098L5530 1071M5560 1098L5543 1064M5560 1098L5541 1060M5560 1098L5709 970M5560 1098L5709 970M5560 1098L5579 1111"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5530 1071h0M5543 1064h0M5541 1060h0M5709 970h0M5709 970h0M5579 1111h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5560 1098h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5560 1098h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mystras. <a href="/atlas?year=1404&amp;focus=despotate-of-the-morea">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
